Now I can see the issue clearly. The old code used byte positions from `text.to_lowercase() to slice the original text` — this is a classic Unicode safety bug. If to_lowercase() changes byte lengths (e.g., Turkish İ → i̇), the byte offsets would be wrong, causing either incorrect highlighting or a panic.

The new code:
1. Case-sensitive path: Direct byte slicing is safe (no case transformation)
2. Case-insensitive path: Builds a char-level mapping between original and lowercased text, then matches at the char level and maps back to original byte offsets

This is exactly the kind of safety issue documented in the CLAUDE.md safety rules: "Never use byte indexing on strings."

